nergy", "title": "Fortune Sector Leaders: Energy", "year": 2024, "rank": 24, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2024, "rank": 187, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2024, "rank": 119, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2024, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2023, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2023, "rank": 176, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2023, "rank": 218, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2022, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 391, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 99, "itemDescription": "<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Chicago-headquartered Exelon, the largest electric company in the U.S., dropped seven spots on this year\u2019s list. In 2021, the company\u2019s profits also decreased 13.1% year over year to $1.7 billion, after falling 33% in 2020. Some observers expect better performance from the company over the coming year after the February 2022 finalization of its separation from Constellation Energy Corp., which handles power generation; the split could allow Exelon to focus more\u00a0</span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">efficiently on energy transmission and distribution.</span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2021, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 369, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;It has been a bumpy year for energy players like Exelon, one of the largest power generators in Texas. Most recently, the company had to contend with damage and delays caused by winter storms in that state, which overwhelmed the electric grid and disrupted millions of lives. The Chicago-based company\u2019s revenue came in at $33 billion, down 4% year over year, and net income was nearly $2 billion, a decrease of 33% from the year before. And there are more changes on the horizon: Exelon has announced that it will separate its utilities businesses from Generation, its power generation division, creating two publicly traded companies.&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:14721,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;14&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:0},&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:12}\">It has been a bumpy year for energy players like Exelon, one of the largest power generators in Texas. Most recently, the company had to contend with damage and delays caused by winter storms in that state, which overwhelmed the electric grid and disrupted millions of lives. The Chicago-based company\u2019s revenue came in at $33 billion, down 4% year over year, and net income was nearly $2 billion, a decrease of 33% from the year before. And there are more changes on the horizon: Exelon has announced that it will separate its utilities businesses from Generation, its power generation division, creating two publicly traded companies.</span>"}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 92, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;It has been a bumpy year for energy players like Exelon, one of the largest power generators in Texas. Most recently, the company had to contend with damage and delays caused by winter storms in that state, which overwhelmed the electric grid and disrupted millions of lives. The Chicago-based company\u2019s revenue came in at $33 billion, down 4% year over year, and net income was nearly $2 billion, a decrease of 33% from the year before. And there are more changes on the horizon: Exelon has announced that it will separate its utilities businesses from Generation, its power generation division, creating two publicly traded companies.&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:14721,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;14&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:0},&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:12}\">It has been a bumpy year for energy players like Exelon, one of the largest power generators in Texas. Most recently, the company had to contend with damage and delays caused by winter storms in that state, which overwhelmed the electric grid and disrupted millions of lives. The Chicago-based company\u2019s revenue came in at $33 billion, down 4% year over year, and net income was nearly $2 billion, a decrease of 33% from the year before. And there are more changes on the horizon: Exelon has announced that it will separate its utilities businesses from Generation, its power generation division, creating two publicly traded companies.</span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2020, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 364, "itemDescription": "The 133-year-old energy holding company had an up-and-down 2019. Exelon\u2019s revenue slipped 4.3% last year, down to $34.4 billion from nearly $36 billion the year before. Net income, however, surged an impressive 46%. (For that, you can credit higher utility earnings, made possible by regulatory rate increases across the majority of Exelon\u2019s divisions, including Baltimore Gas and Electric.) The Chicago-based energy giant remains a powerful force in generation, transmission, and distribution of electricity and natural gas."}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 95, "itemDescription": "The 133-year-old energy holding company had an up and down 2019. Exelon\u2019s revenue slipped 4.3% last year, down to $34.4 billion from nearly $36 billion the year before. Net income, however, surged an impressive 46%. (For that, you can credit higher utility earnings, made possible by regulatory rate increases across the majority of Exelon\u2019s divisions, including Baltimore Gas and Electric.) The Chicago-based energy giant remains a powerful force in generation, transmission, and distribution of electricity and natural gas."}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2019, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2019, "rank": 344, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2019, "rank": 93, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2018, "rank": 356, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2018, "rank": 92, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2017, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2017, "rank": 344, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2017, "rank": 89, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2016, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2016, "rank": 355, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2016, "rank": 95, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Ad